<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guru Arjan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1563">1563</a>–<a href="/years/1606">1606</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Guru Arjan was the fifth of the ten <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> Gurus, born in Goindval in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, the youngest son of Guru Ram Das, whom he succeeded in 1581. He compiled the hymns of the earlier Gurus and other saints into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Adi Granth</a>, the first edition of the Sikh scripture, completed the Darbar Sahib at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amritsar">Amritsar</a>, and is credited with founding Kartarpur, Hargobindpur and Tarn Taran. He asked Sikhs to give a tenth of their income, the dasvandh, which financed gurdwaras and langars. Arrested on the orders of the Mughal Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jahangir">Jahangir</a>, who accused him of supporting a rebellion under Khusrau Mirza, he was tortured and executed in 1606 after refusing to convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guru-arjan.jpg" alt="Guru Arjan" width="330" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Family workshop of Purkhu of Kangra · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guru_Arjan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The growing community attracted the suspicion of the Mughal authorities, and in 1606 <b>Guru Arjan</b> was executed under the emperor Jahangir, becoming the first Sikh martyr.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1604 <b>Guru Arjan</b>, the fifth Sikh Guru, placed hymns of Kabir, Namdev, Ravidas and other saints in the Adi Granth.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Guru Arjan</b>, the fifth Sikh guru, completed the compilation of the Adi Granth, the first rendition of the Sikh scripture later known as the Guru Granth Sahib.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-29">29 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Goindwal Sahib, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.37,75.15&amp;year=1590" aria-label="Goindwal Sahib on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6490 1021 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Guru Arjan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6940 1291h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6940 1291h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.37,75.15&amp;year=1590">What was here in 1590? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
